WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.767
Imagine a team revises the service-planning notice and asks

2
00:00:03.789 --> 00:00:06.456
for feedback only after the new version is approved

3
00:00:06.478 --> 00:00:10.878
and distributed. People may offer useful observations, but they

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.011
have little opportunity to influence the original decision. Participation

5
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.344
has arrived too late.

6
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:20.911
Standards twelve and thirteen address understanding community assets and

7
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:24.267
needs and partnering with communities in the design, implementation

8
00:00:24.578 --> 00:00:28.089
and evaluation of practice. For your individual work, the

9
00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:30.600
question is how you can support an appropriate process

10
00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:33.844
for learning with people affected, within your responsibilities.

11
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.433
This lesson does not require you to organize a

12
00:00:37.456 --> 00:00:41.056
group or personally contact service recipients. You can complete

13
00:00:41.078 --> 00:00:45.233
every activity independently. Where engagement is needed, identify the

14
00:00:45.256 --> 00:00:47.856
established route and the people responsible for it.

15
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.578
Before asking for input, describe the decision. What is

16
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.333
open to change? What requirements or constraints must remain?

17
00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:59.511
How will input be considered, who will decide, and

18
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:01.156
when will people hear what happened?

19
00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:04.194
For the notice, the team may be able to

20
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:08.106
change the wording, layout and explanation of assistance, while

21
00:01:08.128 --> 00:01:11.506
a required time limit remains fixed. Say that clearly.

22
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:14.461
Asking people to choose something that is not actually

23
00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:18.328
open can damage trust. A constrained decision can still

24
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:22.217
benefit from meaningful participation when the boundaries are honest.

25
00:01:22.872 --> 00:01:26.694
Also ask whose experience is missing. People who readily

26
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:29.672
respond to an online survey may not represent those

27
00:01:29.694 --> 00:01:33.339
who had difficulty accessing the original information. A single

28
00:01:33.361 --> 00:01:35.917
advisor cannot be assumed to speak for every person

29
00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:39.628
with a disability, every language community or every family.

30
00:01:40.794 --> 00:01:46.439
Consider the format, language, communication support, timing, location or

31
00:01:46.461 --> 00:01:50.194
technology, and the effort required. An invitation that is

32
00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:53.372
inaccessible can reproduce the very problem the project hopes

33
00:01:53.394 --> 00:01:56.817
to address. Offer appropriate ways to participate through the

34
00:01:56.839 --> 00:01:58.194
approved engagement process.

35
00:01:58.806 --> 00:02:02.583
Respect a person's choice not to participate. Avoid making

36
00:02:02.606 --> 00:02:06.517
access to services appear dependent on providing feedback. Explain

37
00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:09.517
what information is requested, how it will be used

38
00:02:09.606 --> 00:02:13.517
and any limits on confidentiality. Follow applicable privacy and

39
00:02:13.539 --> 00:02:17.894
records requirements. If compensation or reimbursement is available, use

40
00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:20.783
the established rules; do not promise a payment you

41
00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:21.694
cannot authorize.

42
00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:26.172
Participation should make room for the person themselves, including

43
00:02:26.194 --> 00:02:30.083
self-advocates and their chosen supports. Do not automatically substitute

44
00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:33.172
the views of professionals or family members for the

45
00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:36.883
person's perspective. At the same time, different perspectives can

46
00:02:36.906 --> 00:02:39.328
reveal different parts of an experience when their roles

47
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:39.861
are clear.

48
00:02:40.872 --> 00:02:45.517
Community assessment includes strengths. Ask what already works, which

49
00:02:45.539 --> 00:02:48.961
communication approaches are trusted, and what people would want

50
00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:52.828
preserved. A redesign can unintentionally remove a feature that

51
00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.250
some people depend on.

52
00:02:54.794 --> 00:02:58.017
In our example, a clear telephone option may be

53
00:02:58.039 --> 00:03:00.706
valuable even if a new online response form is

54
00:03:00.728 --> 00:03:05.039
introduced. Improving one route does not necessarily justify removing

55
00:03:05.061 --> 00:03:08.794
another. Look for choices that support participation rather than

56
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:10.772
assuming one channel will work for everyone.

57
00:03:11.398 --> 00:03:14.864
When feedback differs, describe the difference rather than forcing

58
00:03:14.887 --> 00:03:18.553
agreement. One person may prefer a brief notice, while

59
00:03:18.576 --> 00:03:22.287
another needs more explanation. A concise main message with

60
00:03:22.309 --> 00:03:25.731
accessible supporting detail might address both needs, but the

61
00:03:25.753 --> 00:03:27.664
proposed solution still needs checking.

62
00:03:28.787 --> 00:03:32.520
After input is considered, explain what changed, what did

63
00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:36.076
not change and why. Be specific without exposing personal

64
00:03:36.098 --> 00:03:39.853
information. If a suggestion cannot be adopted, acknowledge it

65
00:03:39.876 --> 00:03:43.231
and explain the relevant constraint or next step. Silence

66
00:03:43.253 --> 00:03:45.920
can leave people unsure whether their contribution mattered.

67
00:03:46.531 --> 00:03:50.998
Partnership continues into implementation and evaluation. People can help

68
00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.687
identify whether the revised notice actually improves understanding or

69
00:03:54.709 --> 00:03:57.553
whether a new barrier has appeared. That is different

70
00:03:57.576 --> 00:04:00.220
from using a consultation only to endorse a finished

71
00:04:00.242 --> 00:04:00.776
product.

72
00:04:01.876 --> 00:04:05.053
For your work example, write the decision that needs

73
00:04:05.076 --> 00:04:08.720
input, the perspectives that may be missing, an accessible

74
00:04:08.742 --> 00:04:11.987
way to obtain that input, and the role responsible

75
00:04:12.009 --> 00:04:15.387
for arranging it. Then describe how people would hear

76
00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:15.733
back.

77
00:04:16.233 --> 00:04:20.033
If you lack authority to initiate engagement, your action

78
00:04:20.056 --> 00:04:23.322
might be a recommendation to the responsible person. It

79
00:04:23.344 --> 00:04:26.656
can still be precise: ask for input before approval,

80
00:04:26.967 --> 00:04:30.411
identify the access considerations, and explain how the input

81
00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:31.633
could improve the decision.

82
00:04:32.556 --> 00:04:35.844
In the practice activity, choose an approach that gives

83
00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:38.867
people a real opportunity to influence the work while

84
00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:42.711
being honest about constraints. The aim is not simply

85
00:04:42.733 --> 00:04:45.622
to count participants. It is to improve the decision

86
00:04:45.644 --> 00:04:48.244
and the experience through meaningful involvement.

87
00:04:49.171 --> 00:04:52.438
Next, we will consider how to tell whether the

88
00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.260
change is helping. Your plan for participation will be

89
00:04:56.282 --> 00:04:57.371
part of that evidence.
